<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chiang Kai-shek</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1887">1887</a>–<a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chiang Kai-shek was a Chinese military and political leader who headed the Nationalist Party, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>. Commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-expedition">Northern Expedition</a>, he established the Nationalist Government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> in 1928, fought the Communists and regional rivals, and led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1937. Defeated in the civil war, he moved the government of the Republic of China to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in 1949 and ruled there until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek.jpg" alt="Chiang Kai-shek" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chiang_Kai-shek%EF%BC%88%E8%94%A3%E4%B8%AD%E6%AD%A3%EF%BC%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chiang was born on 31 October 1887 in Xikou, then a small village about 48 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningbo">Ningbo</a>, into a family of merchants. His father ran the family salt business, and his mother was a devout Buddhist, the daughter of a Buddhist monk. He received a military education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, settling in Tokyo for its reputation as the centre of Japanese military science and for its exiled Chinese revolutionaries. There he became close to Chen Qimei, who recommended him for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongmenghui">Tongmenghui</a>; he swore allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-principles-of-the-people">Three Principles of the People</a> in 1908.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1920 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> appointed Chiang chief of staff of the Second Guangdong Army, and from 1924 he was commandant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whampoa-military-academy">Whampoa Military Academy</a>. After Sun died on 12 March 1925, Chiang became one of the principal figures in a struggle for the succession in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>. He was appointed commander-in-chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-expedition">Northern Expedition</a> in June 1926, and by the end of October its army had occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuhan">Wuhan</a>. On 12 April troops and armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-gang">Green Gang</a> groups attacked Communist-aligned workers' organisations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, killing resisters and arresting many. Chiang proclaimed a rival Nationalist government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> on 18 April.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian-incident">Xi'an Incident</a> of 1936 forced him into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-united-front">Second United Front</a> with the Communists. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a> broke out in July 1937 he sent 600,000 of his best soldiers to defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, losing over 200,000 men. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> fell in December, and he moved the government inland, first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuhan">Wuhan</a> and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a>. In 1949 his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>'s Communists and retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, where he resumed his duties as president on 1 March 1950.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On Taiwan Chiang imposed martial law and a White Terror, which lasted until 1987 and 1992 respectively. He remained president of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> until his death on 5 April 1975, presiding over land reform and economic growth. Supporters credit him with unifying the nation and leading the resistance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Critics fault him for early appeasement of Japan, the deliberate flooding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, corruption and authoritarian rule on both the mainland and Taiwan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chiang Kai-shek” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With Soviet advice and arms Sun Yat-sen founded the Whampoa Military Academy near Guangzhou in 1924, headed by <b>Chiang Kai-shek</b>, whose National Revolutionary Army launched the Northern Expedition in 1926 and entered Beijing in 1928.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1946 it sent missions to China and India, the first carrying a letter to <b>Chiang Kai-shek</b> that promised to maintain Tibet's independence, and in 1947 a delegation attended the Asian Relations Conference in New Delhi, perhaps the first public appearance of the Tibetan national flag.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet (1912-1951) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Chiang Kai-shek</b>, a Confucian and nationalist, condemned its iconoclasm, and under him the Kuomintang’s New Life Movement promoted Confucianism and brought it into the schools.</blockquote><a href="/movement/may-fourth-movement">May Fourth Movement · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In March 1943, during the war against the Japanese invasion, <b>Chiang Kai-shek</b>, leader of the Kuomintang, published China's Destiny, which argued for one doctrine and one party and stated that without the Kuomintang there would be no China.</blockquote><a href="/songs/without-the-communist-party-there-would-be-no-new-china">Without the Communist Party, There Would Be No New China</a></li><li><blockquote>An uprising in Taiwan against the Kuomintang-led government of the Republic of China was crushed under governor Chen Yi and president <b>Chiang Kai-shek</b>; an estimated 18,000 to 28,000 civilians died.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-28">28 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Returning in 1923, Sun accepted Soviet aid, admitted Communists to his party and founded the Whampoa Military Academy under <b>Chiang Kai-shek</b> in 1924.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-military-government">Canton Military Government</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Xikou, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taipei">Taipei</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.69,121.28&amp;year=1944" aria-label="Xikou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7706 1079 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Chiang Kai-shek"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8156 1349h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8156 1349h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.69,121.28&amp;year=1944">What was here in 1944? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
